    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[SEE_TABLE:9.1]] consider the fraction of segregating sites as S/L; the nucleotide diversity as $ \\pi = \\Pi/L $; and the fraction of sites that are singletons as $ S_1/L $. When searching for genomic regions under selection, our goal shifts to $ \\theta_L = 4N_e\\mu_L $, the corresponding value for the region, where $ \\mu_L = L\\mu $ is the total mutation rate over the L sites. Because the focus in this section is on specific regions, in an effort to keep the notation simple, we suppress the subscript and use $ \\theta $ and $ \\mu $ to denote the region-side values ($ \\theta_L $, $ \\mu_L $).",
